,"severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"Syssitia are men’s communal barracks where husbands slept.","truth":"Syssitia were compulsory communal meals or dining groups for Spartan male citizens. Plutarch describes younger husbands returning to sleep with other young men, not sleeping in a syssition.","evidence":"Plutarch, Lycurgus 15.3, fetched from Perseus; Oxford Classical Dictionary definition of agōgē context","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F6","lesson":3,"field":"content_html","severity":"HIGH","claim":"The Hegeso Stele’s sculptor is unknown.","truth":"The specification identifies the Hegeso Stele as attributed to Callimachus.","evidence":"specs/ocr/classical-civilisation-J199.md, prescribed visual/material sources, p. 16","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F7","lesson":3,"field":"content_html","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"The gynaikon was typically a fixed women’s section at the back or upper floor of an Athenian house.","truth":"Option 1: retain this as a literary ideal. Option 2: revise the lesson, glossary and questions to say that gynaikon could denote women’s household space, while archaeology does not establish a standard separate back or upper-floor women’s quarter.","evidence":"Oxford Academic, Household and City Organization at Olynthus; Cambridge, Domestic Comedy and the Classical Greek House; American School of Classical Studies at Athens, Women in the Athenian Agora","verdict":"ADJUDICATE"},{"id":"F8","lesson":3,"field":"content_html","severity":"LOW","claim":"“I hold here the dear child of my daughter, whom I held on my knees when we were alive and looked on the light of the sun; now dead, I hold it dead.”","truth":"The fetched Metropolitan Museum source gives a materially similar but differently worded translation of the Ampharete inscription. Do not replace this quotation without selecting and licensing a specific translation.","evidence":"Metropolitan Museum of Art, Fragments of the marble stele of a woman holding a baby","verdict":"NOTE"},{"id":"F9","lesson":4,"field":"content_html","severity":"HIGH","claim":"Athenian women entirely lacked property rights.","truth":"Athenian women could hold and inherit property, but a kyrios generally managed it and their control was more restricted than men’s or Spartan women’s.","evidence":"Perseus, Thomas R. Martin, Property, Social Freedom, and Athenian Women","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F10","lesson":4,"field":"content_html","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"Syssitia were communal dining and sleeping barracks, and a husband’s sleeping arrangement after marriage is identified as syssitia.","truth":"Syssitia were communal meals or dining groups. Younger husbands returned to communal quarters after visiting their wives; the two should not be equated.","evidence":"Plutarch, Lycurgus 15.3, fetched from Perseus","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F11","lesson":4,"field":"content_html","severity":"LOW","claim":"“come back with your shield or on it” is presented as a Spartan saying.","truth":"The fetched Plutarch collection gives the saying as “Either this or upon this.” The lesson’s wording is a familiar paraphrase, not the fetched translation; retain no exact quotation unless a chosen translation is verified.","evidence":"Plutarch, Sayings of Spartan Women 16, fetched from University of Chicago Penelope","verdict":"NOTE"},{"id":"F12","lesson":5,"field":"content_html","severity":"HIGH","claim":"Patria potestas gave the paterfamilias legal authority over his wife, children and all household slaves.","truth":"Patria potestas concerned the paterfamilias’s children and descendants. A wife came under a husband’s manus only in a cum manu marriage; slavery was not patria potestas.","evidence":"Roman-law definitions of patria potestas and manus; specification p. 13 distinguishes cum manu and sine manu marriage","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F13","lesson":5,"field":"content_html","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"The so-called Sappho fresco depicts a wealthy, educated Roman woman and proves that education was part of the ideal for wealthy women.","truth":"Option 1: describe only the visible tablet and stylus, and say the subject’s identity is unknown. Option 2: present education as one interpretation, alongside scholarship that treats the figure as an idealised or Muse-like image rather than a portrait of a real educated woman.","evidence":"Smarthistory, Portrait of a Young Woman (known as Sappho); American Journal of Archaeology, Writing Paraphernalia, Tablets, and Muses in Campanian Wall Painting","verdict":"ADJUDICATE"}],"lessons_clean":[],"summary":"Five lessons were checked. Eleven verified issues have surgical edits; two interpretation issues require editorial judgement, and two quotations are recorded without replacement."}

