+  {"lesson":2,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[3].a","expect":"Her childhood toys, her kalyptra (bridal veil) and often a lock of her hair — marking the end of girlhood.","value":"Her childhood toys and often a lock of hair, marking the end of girlhood.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F3: The fetched wedding source supports toys and a hair offering, not dedication of the veil."},
+  {"lesson":2,"field":"glossary_terms","path":"[2].definition","expect":"The unveiling ceremony at a Greek wedding: the ritual act in which the groom lifted the bride's kalyptra (veil). The word can also refer to the gifts exchanged at this moment. The veil itself was called the kalyptra, which the bride dedicated to Artemis before the wedding.","value":"The unveiling ceremony at a Greek wedding: the groom lifted the bride’s kalyptra (veil). The word can also refer to gifts exchanged at this moment. The veil itself was called the kalyptra.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F3: Remove the unsupported claim that the bridal veil was dedicated to Artemis."},
+  {"lesson":2,"field":"glossary_terms","path":"[3].definition","expect":"The bridal veil worn by an Athenian bride, dedicated to Artemis before the wedding as part of the bride's transition from girlhood. The ceremonial lifting of the kalyptra by the groom was called the anakalypteria.","value":"The bridal veil worn by an Athenian bride. The groom’s ceremonial lifting of it was called the anakalypteria.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F3: The veil was used in the unveiling ritual, not evidenced as an offering."},
+  {"lesson":2,"field":"content_html","find":"Spartan women could own property and speak publicly; Athenian women could not.","replace":"Spartan women had wider property rights and greater public visibility than Athenian women.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F4: Athenian women could hold and inherit property, although their control was restricted."},
