+[
+  {"lesson":1,"field":"content_html","find":"The prescribed literary source for Helen is Euripides&rsquo; play <em>Helen</em>, lines 1&ndash;67, in which Helen herself speaks. In Homer&rsquo;s <em>Iliad</em>, Book 3 (lines 121&ndash;244), she appears","replace":"The prescribed literary source for Helen is Euripides&rsquo; play <em>Helen</em>, lines 1&ndash;67, in which Helen herself speaks. In Homer&rsquo;s <em>Iliad</em>, Book 3 (lines 121&ndash;180), she appears","severity":"HIGH","note":"F1: The prescribed Iliad passage ends at line 180."},
+  {"lesson":1,"field":"conclusion_html","find":"Homer&rsquo;s <em>Iliad</em> Book 3 (lines 121&ndash;244 for the teichoscopia)","replace":"Homer&rsquo;s <em>Iliad</em> Book 3 (lines 121&ndash;180 for the teichoscopia)","severity":"HIGH","note":"F1: The prescribed Iliad passage ends at line 180."},
+  {"lesson":1,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[6].a","expect":"She was crushed to death by the Sabines' shields.","value":"She demanded what the Sabines wore on their arms, but they crushed her with shields.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F2: The answer must address both parts of the question."},
+
+  {"lesson":2,"field":"content_html","find":"Before the ceremony, the bride dedicated her childhood toys, her <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"The bridal veil worn by an Athenian bride; dedicated to Artemis before the wedding as part of the transition from girlhood. The ceremonial lifting of the veil by the groom was known as the anakalypteria (the unveiling).\">kalyptra</dfn> (bridal veil) and often a lock of her hair to the goddess Artemis, marking the end of girlhood.","replace":"Before the ceremony, the bride dedicated her childhood toys and often a lock of her hair to the goddess Artemis, marking the end of girlhood. She wore a <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"The bridal veil worn by an Athenian bride. The ceremonial lifting of the veil by the groom was known as the anakalypteria (the unveiling).\">kalyptra</dfn> (bridal veil) during the wedding.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F3: The kalyptra was worn in the unveiling ritual, not listed as an offering to Artemis."},
+  {"lesson":2,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[3].a","expect":"Her childhood toys, her kalyptra (bridal veil) and often a lock of her hair — marking the end of girlhood.","value":"Her childhood toys and often a lock of hair, marking the end of girlhood.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F3: The fetched wedding source supports toys and a hair offering, not dedication of the veil."},
+  {"lesson":2,"field":"glossary_terms","path":"[2].definition","expect":"The unveiling ceremony at a Greek wedding: the ritual act in which the groom lifted the bride's kalyptra (veil). The word can also refer to the gifts exchanged at this moment. The veil itself was called the kalyptra, which the bride dedicated to Artemis before the wedding.","value":"The unveiling ceremony at a Greek wedding: the groom lifted the bride’s kalyptra (veil). The word can also refer to gifts exchanged at this moment. The veil itself was called the kalyptra.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F3: Remove the unsupported claim that the bridal veil was dedicated to Artemis."},
+  {"lesson":2,"field":"glossary_terms","path":"[3].definition","expect":"The bridal veil worn by an Athenian bride, dedicated to Artemis before the wedding as part of the bride's transition from girlhood. The ceremonial lifting of the kalyptra by the groom was called the anakalypteria.","value":"The bridal veil worn by an Athenian bride. The groom’s ceremonial lifting of it was called the anakalypteria.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F3: The veil was used in the unveiling ritual, not evidenced as an offering."},
+  {"lesson":2,"field":"content_html","find":"Spartan women could own property and speak publicly; Athenian women could not.","replace":"Spartan women had wider property rights and greater public visibility than Athenian women.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F4: Athenian women could hold and inherit property, although their control was restricted."},
+  {"lesson":2,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[10].a","expect":"Spartan women could own and inherit property.","value":"Spartan women had wider rights to own and inherit property than Athenian women.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F4: Avoid the false implication that Athenian women could not hold property."},
+  {"lesson":2,"field":"content_html","find":"the husband continued to sleep in the men&rsquo;s communal barracks (the syssitia) and visited his wife in secret","replace":"the husband continued to sleep in communal quarters and visited his wife in secret","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F5: Syssitia were communal meals or dining groups, not barracks."},
+  {"lesson":2,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[9].q","expect":"Which Spartan institution required married men to continue sleeping away from home?","value":"What did younger Spartan husbands return to after visiting their wives?","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F5: Syssitia did not designate sleeping quarters."},
+  {"lesson":2,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[9].a","expect":"The syssitia — the men's communal barracks.","value":"They returned to communal quarters with other young men.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F5: Correct the sleeping arrangement without calling syssitia barracks."},
+  {"lesson":2,"field":"glossary_terms","path":"[5].definition","expect":"The communal dining and sleeping barracks for Spartan male citizens; married Spartan men continued to live here rather than immediately with their wives.","value":"The communal dining groups for Spartan male citizens. Younger husbands continued to return to communal quarters after marriage.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F5: Syssitia were dining groups, not sleeping barracks."},
+
+  {"lesson":3,"field":"content_html","find":"<dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"A marble grave relief (stele), c. 400 BC, in the National Archaeological Museum Athens (no. 3624), depicting a seated woman examining jewellery held by a standing slave girl. The sculptor is unknown.\">Hegeso Stele</dfn>","replace":"<dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"A marble grave relief (stele), c. 400 BC, in the National Archaeological Museum Athens (no. 3624), depicting a seated woman examining jewellery held by a standing slave girl. It is attributed to Callimachus.\">Hegeso Stele</dfn>","severity":"HIGH","note":"F6: The specification attributes the Hegeso Stele to Callimachus."},
+  {"lesson":3,"field":"practice_questions","path":"[1].marks","expect":"1 mark each for any three of: it is a grave relief (stele); it shows a seated woman reaching into or looking at a jewellery box; a standing slave girl holds the box; the woman is elegantly dressed; the sculptor is unknown; it is dated to around 400 BC; it is in the National Archaeological Museum in Athens (no. 3624).","value":"1 mark each for any three of: it is a grave relief (stele); it shows a seated woman reaching into or looking at a jewellery box; a standing slave girl holds the box; the woman is elegantly dressed; it is attributed to Callimachus; it is dated to around 400 BC; it is in the National Archaeological Museum in Athens (no. 3624).","severity":"HIGH","note":"F6: Correct the prescribed source’s attribution."},
+  {"lesson":3,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[0].a","expect":"A grave relief (c. 400 BC) by an unknown sculptor, in the National Archaeological Museum, Athens (no. 3624), depicting a seated woman examining jewellery held by a slave girl.","value":"A c. 400 BC grave relief attributed to Callimachus, in the National Archaeological Museum, Athens (no. 3624), showing Hegeso with a slave girl.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F6: Correct the prescribed source’s attribution."},
+  {"lesson":3,"field":"glossary_terms","path":"[3].definition","expect":"A grave relief (c. 400 BC) by an unknown sculptor, in the National Archaeological Museum Athens (no. 3624), showing a seated woman examining jewellery held by a slave; a prescribed visual source for the Athenian domestic ideal.","value":"A grave relief (c. 400 BC) attributed to Callimachus, in the National Archaeological Museum Athens (no. 3624), showing a seated woman examining jewellery held by a slave; a prescribed visual source for the Athenian domestic ideal.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F6: Correct the prescribed source’s attribution."},
+
+  {"lesson":4,"field":"content_html","find":"Spartan women had property rights that Athenian women entirely lacked. They could own, inherit and manage property in their own right.","replace":"Spartan women had wider property rights than Athenian women. They could own, inherit and manage property in their own right.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F9: Athenian women could hold and inherit property, though their control was restricted."},
+  {"lesson":4,"field":"conclusion_html","find":"Athenian women had none of these rights.","replace":"Athenian women had more limited property rights and public roles.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F9: Athenian women did have restricted property rights."},
+  {"lesson":4,"field":"practice_questions","path":"[3].marks","expect":"1 mark each for any four of: Spartan men lived in communal barracks until around age thirty — their wives needed to manage estates independently; the male Spartan citizen population declined due to military losses, leaving estates to female heirs; Sparta's system required wives to be economically capable; Athenian law did not allow women to own property — the kyrios system meant all property was managed by the male guardian; Spartan women's property rights enabled them to keep estates running while men were on campaign.","value":"1 mark each for any four of: Spartan men lived in communal barracks until around age thirty — their wives needed to manage estates independently; the male Spartan citizen population declined due to military losses, leaving estates to female heirs; Sparta’s system required wives to be economically capable; Athenian women could hold property, but a kyrios generally managed it; Spartan women’s property rights enabled them to keep estates running while men were on campaign.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F9: Correct the false claim that Athenian women could not own property."},
+  {"lesson":4,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[7].a","expect":"Spartan women could own and inherit property; Athenian women could not.","value":"Spartan women had wider rights to own and inherit property than Athenian women.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F9: Athenian women could hold and inherit property under restrictions."},
+  {"lesson":4,"field":"content_html","find":"they slept in the men&rsquo;s communal barracks (the <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"The communal dining and sleeping barracks where Spartan male citizens lived until around age thirty, even after marriage; they visited their wives in secret during this period.\">syssitia</dfn>).","replace":"younger husbands returned to communal quarters after visiting their wives. The <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"The compulsory communal meals attended by Spartan male citizens.\">syssitia</dfn> were their dining groups.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F10: Syssitia were communal meals or dining groups, not sleeping barracks."},
+  {"lesson":4,"field":"content_html","find":"Spartan husbands lived in the men&rsquo;s communal barracks (syssitia) until around age thirty, even after marriage.","replace":"Spartan husbands continued to return to communal quarters until around age thirty, even after marriage.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F10: Do not call the syssitia sleeping barracks."},
+  {"lesson":4,"field":"conclusion_html","find":"while husbands lived in the syssitia (men&rsquo;s barracks);","replace":"while younger husbands returned to communal quarters;","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F10: Syssitia were communal meals, not barracks."},
+  {"lesson":4,"field":"practice_questions","path":"[0].text","expect":"Name the communal male institution where Spartan husbands slept even after marriage.","value":"Name the compulsory communal meals attended by Spartan male citizens.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F10: Correct the definition of syssitia."},
+  {"lesson":4,"field":"practice_questions","path":"[0].marks","expect":"1 mark for: syssitia (accept 'the communal barracks' or 'the men's dining halls').","value":"1 mark for: syssitia (accept ‘the communal dining groups’ or ‘the common meals’).","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F10: Syssitia were dining groups, not barracks."},
+  {"lesson":4,"field":"knowledge_checks","path":"[2].q","expect":"Spartan husbands lived in the men's communal _____ until around age thirty, even after they were married.","value":"Spartan husbands continued to attend communal _____ even after they were married.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F10: The syssitia were meals, so the question must not call them sleeping quarters."},
+  {"lesson":4,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[2].a","expect":"The communal dining and sleeping barracks where Spartan male citizens lived until around age thirty.","value":"The compulsory communal meals and dining groups for Spartan male citizens.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F10: Correct the definition of syssitia."},
+  {"lesson":4,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[3].a","expect":"Their husbands lived in the men's barracks (syssitia) and were often absent on campaign.","value":"Their husbands returned to communal quarters and were often absent on campaign.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F10: Do not equate syssitia with barracks."},
+  {"lesson":4,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[9].q","expect":"At what approximate age did Spartan men begin living with their wives rather than in the barracks?","value":"At what approximate age did Spartan men begin living with their wives?","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F10: Remove the false identification of syssitia as barracks."},
+  {"lesson":4,"field":"glossary_terms","path":"[1].definition","expect":"The communal dining and sleeping barracks where Spartan male citizens lived until around age thirty, even after marriage.","value":"The compulsory communal meals and dining groups for Spartan male citizens.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F10: Syssitia were dining groups, not sleeping barracks."},
+
+  {"lesson":5,"field":"content_html","find":"<dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"The male head of the Roman household; in Roman law he held legal power (patria potestas) over all members of his family, including the right to accept or expose newborn children.\">paterfamilias</dfn>","replace":"<dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"The male head of a Roman family. He held patria potestas over his children and descendants, including the right to accept or expose newborn children.\">paterfamilias</dfn>","severity":"HIGH","note":"F12: Patria potestas did not itself extend to a wife or describe ownership of slaves."},
+  {"lesson":5,"field":"content_html","find":"In Roman law, the paterfamilias held legal authority (patria potestas) over his wife, children and all household slaves. In theory, this power was absolute.","replace":"In Roman law, the paterfamilias held patria potestas over his children and descendants. A wife came under his manus only in a cum manu marriage. In theory, this power was absolute.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F12: Distinguish patria potestas from manus."},
+  {"lesson":5,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[7].a","expect":"The male head of household who held legal authority (patria potestas) over all family members.","value":"The male head of a Roman family, with patria potestas over his children and descendants.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F12: Patria potestas did not itself give authority over every family member."},
+  {"lesson":5,"field":"glossary_terms","path":"[1].definition","expect":"The male head of the Roman household, who held legal authority (patria potestas) over all members of his family including wife, children and slaves.","value":"The male head of a Roman family, who held patria potestas over his children and descendants.","severity":"HIGH","note":"F12: Patria potestas must be distinguished from manus and slave ownership."}
+]
